== Fluent Talk of Religion

III. It is no sign that affections are truly gracious affections, or that they are not, that they cause those who have them to be fluent, fervent and abundant in talking of the things of religion.

There are many persons who, if they see this in others, are greatly prejudiced against them. To them, others being so full of talk is enough ground to condemn them as Pharisees and showy hypocrites. On the other hand, there are many who, if they see this effect in anyone, are very ignorantly and rashly eager to decide at once that they are the true children of God and are under the saving influences of his Spirit, and they speak of it as a great evidence of a new creature. They say, “Such a one’s mouth is opened now. He used to be slow to speak, but now he is full and free. He is free now to open his heart and tell his experiences and declare the praises of God. It comes from him as freely as water from a fountain,” and the like. And they are especially captivated into a confident and undoubting persuasion that such persons have been savingly worked on if they are not only free and abundant in their talk, but very full of feeling and earnest in it.

But this is the fruit of only a little judgment and of a scanty and short experience, as events abundantly show. It is a mistake persons often fall into through trusting their own wisdom and discernment, and making their own notions their rule instead of the holy Scripture. The Scripture is full of rules, both for how we should judge our own state and for how we should be guided in our opinion of others. Yet nowhere do we have any rule by which to judge ourselves or others to be in a good state from any such effect. For this is only the religion of the mouth and the tongue. It is what the Scripture represents by the leaves of a tree, which, though the tree ought not to be without them, are nowhere given as an evidence of the goodness of the tree.

That persons are inclined to talk abundantly of the things of religion may be from a good cause, and it may be from a bad one. It may be because their hearts are very full of holy affections, for #emph[out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.] And it may be because their hearts are very full of religious affection which is not holy, for still out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks. It is very much the nature of the affections, of whatever kind they are and whatever objects they are exercised about, if they are strong, to incline persons to speak a great deal of what they are affected with, and not only to speak much, but to speak very earnestly and fervently. And therefore persons talking abundantly and very fervently about the things of religion can be evidence of no more than this, that they are very much affected with the things of religion. But this may be so, as has already been shown, and there may be no grace. Whatever men are greatly affected with, they will be earnestly engaged about while the high affection lasts, and they will be likely to show that earnestness in their talk and behavior. So did the greater part of the #emph[Jews] in all #emph[Judea] and #emph[Galilee] for a while, about #emph[John the Baptist’s] preaching and baptism, when they were willing for a season to rejoice in his light. A mighty stir was made all over the land, and among all sorts of persons, about this great prophet and his ministry. And in the same way the multitude often showed great earnestness and a mighty engagement of spirit in everything that was external about Christ and his preaching and miracles. They were #emph[astonished at his teaching, at once receiving the word with joy;] they followed him, sometimes night and day, leaving food, drink and sleep to hear him. Once they followed him into the wilderness, going three days without food, to hear him. At times they praised him to the skies, saying, “Never has anyone spoken like this man!” and they were fervent and earnest in what they said. But what did these things come to in the greater part of them?

A person may be too full of talk about his own experiences, commonly turning to it everywhere and in all company. And when it is so, it is a dark sign rather than a good one. A tree that is too full of leaves seldom bears much fruit. And a cloud, though to all appearance very heavy and full of water, seldom gives much rain to the dry and thirsty earth if it brings too much wind with it. The Holy Spirit is pleased several times to use this very thing to represent a great show of religion with the mouth without fruit in the life to answer it: “Like clouds and wind without rain is the man who boasts of gifts never given” (Prov 25:14). And the apostle #emph[Jude,] speaking of some in the early church who had #emph[crept in unnoticed] among the saints and, having a great show of religion, were for a while not suspected, says, “They are clouds without water, carried along by the wind” (Jude 4, 12). And the apostle #emph[Peter,] speaking of the same people, says, “These men are clouds without water, driven by a storm” (2 Pet 2:17).

False affections, if they are equally strong, are much more eager to declare themselves than true ones. For it is the nature of false religion to seek show and notice, as it was with the Pharisees.#footnote[That famous experiential divine Mr. #emph[Shepard] says, “A Pharisee’s trumpet will be heard to the end of the town, when simplicity walks through the town unseen. That is why a man will at times commend himself in a hidden way (and #emph[myself] always comes in), and tell you a long story of his conversion; and it is a hundred to one that some lie or other slips out with it. Why, the secret meaning is, #emph[Please admire me.] That is why they complain of their shortcomings and weaknesses: #emph[please think what a broken-hearted Christian I am.]” Parab. Of the ten virgins, Part I. Page 179, 180. And holy Mr. #emph[Flavel] says this: “O reader, if your heart were right with God, and you did not cheat yourself with an empty profession of faith, you would have frequent business with God which you would be unwilling for your dearest friend, or the wife of your bosom, to know about. #emph[Non est religio, ubi omnia patent.] Religion does not lie open to all, to the eyes of men. Duties that are seen maintain our reputation, but secret duties maintain our life. It was the saying of a heathen about his secret friendship with his friend, #emph[What need is there for the world to know about it? You and I are theater enough for each other.] There are enclosed pleasures in religion which none but renewed, spiritual souls understand by feeling them.” #emph[Flavel’s] touch-stone of sincerity, ch. 2. Sect. 2.]
